V-race #2 sub-hyp (b) STRONGER-DRAIN probe (firmware-only, NO rebuild): the CHEAP,
free last lever before declaring the ceiling. The bare fence before the eager-V
save/restore only MITIGATES the wild-store fault, consistent with a Saturn busy
signal deasserting before a prior store's mem transaction fully retires -> the
eager-V vse8.v/vle8.v issues into a not-yet-drained VMU and collides (in-range,
8-aligned switch_handle corruption; interrupt-defer proven ineffective, so this is
NOT an interrupt window). This adds z_riscv_v_strong_drain() = fence + a DEPENDENT
load-after-store to a canary (load can't retire until the store is globally visible)
+ fence, called right before the bulk vse8.v (save) and vle8.v (restore), on top of
the current fix set (is_dirty init-guard + vstart/fence). Orthogonal to all RTL.

APPLY: cd <zephyr>; git apply zephyr_v_strong_drain.patch  (already applied to the
local tree v.c; if you build from a different tree, apply there -- not both).
TEST: rebuild FIRMWARE only, multi-run PREEMPTIBLE build_rocc_pre xN (and ATOMIC
build_rocc_gated xN). If the mcause-5 tally IMPROVES markedly -> sub-hyp (b) confirmed
(store-drain-completeness), and we scope a proper barrier / RTL busy-signal fix. If it
does NOT move from ~3-4/8 -> sub-hyp (b) is OUT, and the wild vse8.v store must be
pinned by waveform (xsim); interrupt-defer + drain both exhausted.
NOTE: this is a low-confidence probe -- a scalar canary load/store may not fully drain
the decoupled VECTOR VMU path if their store buffers are separate; it's the cheapest
test of (b) with no rebuild. The wild-thread guard may stay for this run's cross-check
but is dropped from the final set.

